1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Welcome back. Every request you send to Claude runs through a single endpoint,

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,000
and it always has the same shape. Get that shape wrong, and nothing else works.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:14,000
This lesson walks through the turns, the blocks their content carries,

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,000
the required fields, and where the system prompt lives.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Let's start with the fields you can't leave out, because a request fails without them.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:28,000
The first is model. That's the Claude model that answers, named as a string,

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:34,000
and it picks the capability tier your call runs on. The second is max tokens. It caps how many

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,000
tokens the reply is allowed to use, and the response stops once it hits that ceiling.

9
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:46,000
So size it for the output you actually expect. The third is messages. The ordered array of

10
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:52,000
conversation turns. It carries the full exchange the model reasons over. Miss any one of these

11
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,000
three, and the call won't even get off the ground. Everything else you add later is optional on top

12
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:04,000
of them. Now let's look at how those turns line up. The messages array is an ordered list,

13
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:10,000
and each turn is tagged with a role. That role is either user, meaning something you sent,

14
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,000
or assistant, meaning something Claude said back. A couple of rules matter here. First,

15
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,000
the very first turn always has to be a user turn. If you lead with an assistant turn,

16
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:27,000
the call is rejected. Second, if you stack two turns with the same role back-to-back,

17
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:35,000
the API quietly merges them into one. The model reads this order top to bottom to track who said

18
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:41,000
what and when. Keeping the sequence clean is what lets a multi-turn conversation actually make sense

19
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:47,000
to the model. Content comes in two shapes, and knowing when to reach for each keeps your code

20
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:54,000
simple. The plain string form is the simplest. A turn's content is just text, which is perfect for

21
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,000
a quick single message prompt where nothing else is attached. The moment you need more,

22
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,000
you switch to a list of content blocks. Now, content is an array of typed blocks,

23
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:12,000
so a single turn can mix text with images and tool results all together. Here's the useful part.

24
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Both forms are valid on the same endpoint, so you're never picking a different API,

25
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:22,000
just a richer content field. Start with a string when that's all you need, and reach for blocks

26
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:29,000
the moment one turn has to carry more than words. Let's name the block types you'll actually pass.

27
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:35,000
A text block is plain words in a turn, and it's the most common block on both sides of the

28
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:41,000
conversation. An image block is a picture you attach to a user, turn for the model to look at,

29
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:48,000
alongside your text. Then there are the tool blocks. A tool use block is one Claude emits,

30
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,000
naming a tool and the input it wants your code to run. A tool result block is the one you send back,

31
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
carrying that tool's output, and tied to its original request by a matching ID. The point

32
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,000
is that every kind of data rides in the same content list, just under a different block type.

33
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:12,000
This next point trips up a lot of developers, so let's be clear. The Claude API is stateless.

34
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:19,000
It keeps no memory of your past calls. Every bit of conversation state lives in the request you send,

35
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:25,000
which means you resend the full history every single time. The server treats each call as brand

36
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:32,000
new, with no idea what came before, unless you include it. So if you drop earlier turns to save

37
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:38,000
space, you erase what the model knows about the conversation. The transcript is yours to own and

38
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:44,000
carry forward, not something sitting on Anthropic's servers waiting for you. Once this clicks,

39
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:50,000
multi-turn conversations stop feeling mysterious. You're just rebuilding and resending a growing list

40
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:58,000
on every call. So how do you actually preserve that state? You echo prior turns. Each new request,

41
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:04,000
you rebuild the array by appending the last assistant reply and your new user input onto

42
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:11,000
what you already had. The subtle part is how you append. You keep those assistant blocks intact,

43
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:17,000
passing them back as structured blocks, not flattened into a plain string. If you flatten

44
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:23,000
the reply to text, you drop any tool blocks and thinking blocks it contained. Thinking blocks,

45
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:29,000
the extended reasoning you'll meet in the thinking lesson, carry a signature you must pass back

46
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:34,000
untouched. Flatten them away and the very next call breaks because the model goes looking for

47
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:40,000
blocks that are no longer there. So the rule is simple, carry every turn forward in its original

48
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:48,000
block form and the conversation stays coherent. Now, where does the system prompt fit into all this?

49
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:53,000
It sits at the top level of the request, completely separate from the messages array.

50
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Think of it as standing instructions. It sets the model's role, its tone, and the rules it should

51
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:07,000
follow for the whole call and it applies to every turn at once. What it isn't is a user or assistant

52
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:14,000
turn. You don't stuff it into the messages list as its own role. You pass it in its own top level

53
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:20,000
field, either as a plain string or as a list of text blocks when you want finer control. Because

54
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:26,000
it lives outside the back and forth, it frames the entire conversation from above rather than

55
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:32,000
being one more message inside it. Let's put the two side by side because the distinction is easy

56
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:39,000
to blur. The system prompt is top level instruction. It persists across the whole request and shapes

57
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:45,000
how Claude behaves, but it's not part of the turn-by-turn exchange itself. Message content

58
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:52,000
is the actual back and forth. Your user turns carry your inputs. The assistant turns carry

59
00:05:52,000 --> 00:06:00,000
Claude's replies and each one is made of blocks. The model reads in order. So the system prompt

60
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:05,000
answers, how should you behave? While the message content answers, what are we actually talking

61
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:12,000
about? Both travel in the same request, but they play different roles. Keep the durable rules in

62
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:17,000
the system prompt and keep the live conversation in the message content and your requests stay

63
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:25,000
clean and easy to reason about. Let's recap. A Claude request reduces to one core shape.

64
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:30,000
You set the model, max tokens and messages, order the turns so the first is a user turn,

65
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:36,000
and let content be a string or a list of typed blocks with the system prompt at the top level.

66
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Keep in mind the API is stateless, so you resend the full history every single time.

67
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:48,000
That covers the messages API turns, blocks and roles. I'll see you in the next one.

